{
  "bridge_text": "With the name Mosk firmly tucked away in our memories, Lina stood her ground on the hollow steps. The old fox watched her, waiting to see if she would truly turn back.",
  "resume_strategy": "skip",
  "replacement_segments": [
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "text": "Mosk pointed a thin, trembling finger toward the exit. Lina reached into her pocket and pulled out the last apple of the season, setting it gently on the dusty step between them. This is for the trouble of walking me down, she said softly. Mosk stared at the crisp fruit for a long time, his eyes softening. Then he stood up, adjusted his green vest, and stepped onto the first stair without a word."
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "text": "Deep at the bottom of the stairs, the humming grew into a soft, melodic song. A firefly, large as a robin, lay motionless on a bed of velvet moss, her golden glow flickering like a dying candle. Mosk’s whiskers trembled as he looked at her. She had been his friend once, he whispered, his voice cracking with regret. He had failed to bring her medicine during a harsh winter long ago, and he had guarded this tree ever since, ashamed to face the light he had let fade."
    }
  ],
  "active_scene_id": "s3"
